==Solution Alternate==

Key Idea:

Only the <imath>3</imath> x <imath>3</imath> inner grid can initially contain filled squares, since the outer border is empty. We want exactly the center square to be filled after one step.

Case 1: The center is initially filled.

A filled square remains filled precisely when it has <imath>2</imath> or <imath>3</imath> filled neighbors. We examine the possible choices of filled neighbors of the center and require that no other square becomes filled.

This gives <imath>2</imath> valid configurations, counting rotations/reflections.

Case 2: The center is initially empty.

For the center to become filled, it must have exactly <imath>3</imath> filled neighbors. There are several possible shapes for these three neighbors. Counting the configurations of each shape gives

<imath>2 + 4 + 4 + 4 + 8 = 22.</imath>

The total number of configurations is

\begin{align*}
2+(2+4+4+4+8)
&=2+22\\
&=\boxed{24}.
\end{align*}

Therefore the answer is <imath>\boxed{\textbf{(C)}\ 22}.</imath>

